1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. Qg4 Nc6 5. Nf3 Nge7 6. Bd3 Nxd4 7. Nxd4 cxd4 8. O-O Nc6 9. Re1 Qc7 10. Qg3 Bd7 11. a3 O-O-O 12. b4 f6 13. f4 g5 14. exf6 gxf4 15. Bxf4 e5 16. Bxe5 Nxe5 17. Qxe5 Bd6 18. Qxd4 Bxh2+ 19. Kh1 {Position in ILN} Kb8 $4 ({Now} 19... Rde8 {would win the exchange, e.g.} 20. Nd2 (20. Rxe8+ $2 Rxe8 21. Qf2 Bg3) 20... Be5) 20. Nd2 Rhg8 $2 21. f7 $1 Rg4 ({And now} 21... Rxg2 {, which had appeared oh so pretty, fails by a hair's breadth:} 22. Kxg2 Qg3+ (22... Bh3+ 23. Kh1 $18) 23. Kf1 Bh3+ 24. Ke2 Bg4+ {, and now, by} 25. Nf3 {discarding knight, White can reach safety, e.g.} Qxf3+ 26. Kd2 Bf4+ 27. Kc3 Rc8+ 28. Kb3 {; out of all danger at last!}) 22. Qf6 $1 {Basman, brilliantly exploiting his passed f-pawn, gave me no chance.} Rf8 23. Bf5 Rf4 24. Qe7 Rxf7 25. Qxf7 Rxf5 26. Re8+ Bxe8 27. Qxe8+ Qc8 28. Qxc8+ Kxc8 29. Kxh2 {and wins (Sources: University Chess, Summer Term 1965; Illustrated London News, 20 March 1965, but with the colours wrong and at least one error in the score)} 1-0

