1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 a6 3. c3 Nf6 4. e5 Nd5 5. d4 cxd4 6. Qxd4 Nc7 7. Bc4 e6 8. Bf4 Nc6 9. Qb6 Be7 10. Nbd2 O-O 11. Ne4 Ne8 12. Qe3 f6 13. Rd1 fxe5 14. Nxe5 d5 15. Bb3 Qa5 16. Nd2 Bc5 17. Nxc6 bxc6 18. Qg3 Ra7 19. O-O Raf7 20. Be3 Bd6 21. Qh4 Nf6 22. Nf3 Ne4 23. Bc2 Be7 24. Bg5 Bxg5 25. Nxg5 Nxg5 26. Qxg5 Qxa2 27. Rd2 Qa5 28. Rd3 Qb6 29. Rh3 g6 30. Rh6 Rg7 31. h4 Qxb2 32. Bxg6 hxg6 33. h5 Qxc3 34. hxg6 Rf6 35. Qh5 Kf8 36. Rh8+ Ke7 37. Rxc8 Rfxg6 38. Rb1 $4 {(sealed)} (38. Qh4+ $11) 38... Rxg2+ 39. Kh1 Kd6 40. Qh8 Rh2+ $4 ({Here I had intended} 40... Rg1+ 41. Rxg1 Qf3+ 42. Kh2 Qxf2+ 43. Kh3 Qf3+ 44. Kh4 Qe4+ 45. Kh5 Rh7+ {picking up the queen. Then I noticed a quicker win by 40...Rh2+. If White captures with the queen 41...Qf3+ mates. If 41 Kxh2 Qe5+ also mates soon. After checking the variation over I played 40...Rh2+.}) 41. Kxh2 $4 Qe5+ {Why the question marks? Both players had overlooked that 41 Qxh2 is CHECK! (Source: CHESS, October 1965, Vol.31/491, p14)} 0-1

