1. d4 c5 2. d5 d6 3. c4 e5 4. Nc3 g6 5. e4 Bh6 6. Bd3 Bxc1 7. Qxc1 a6 8. Nge2 Qh4 9. g3 Qh3 10. f4 Nd7 11. Qd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O O-O 13. Rdf1 Kg7 14. Ng1 Qh6 15. Nf3 Re8 16. Kb1 exf4 17. gxf4 b5 {(Black's counterattack starts)} 18. cxb5 Nb6 19. Ng5 Ra7 20. bxa6 Bxa6 21. Bxa6 Rxa6 {(Not dissimilar to positions arising from the Benko Gambit, where Black gives up a queen-side pawn in order to attack down the open a- and b-files. The position is finely balanced)} 22. Nb5 $2 {(It seems wrong to take away a defender from the pawn on e4, though Basman's next move is hard to foresee)} (22. Rhg1 {may be the way to proceed and then if} Rb8 {he can attack with} 23. e5 $5) 22... Rxe4 $5 {(Ever the one for the unexpected, Basman produces this exchange sacrifice. I will not pretend I fully understand what it is all about)} 23. Nxe4 Nxe4 24. Qe2 Nc4 {(Part of Basman's motivation becomes clear at least: White cannot capture either knight as the other will fork king and queen with Nd2+. But how does he carry on the attack from here?)} 25. Ka1 $2 (25. Kc2 $5 {or perhaps better 25.Nc3 were called for here. White should still have the edge}) 25... Ned2 26. Na3 $4 (26. Qd3 $1 {was essential, keeping an eye on the b3 square for checks and tying the knights down to mutual defence. And if} Nxf1 27. Qxc4 Nxh2 {(Other knight moves fail to Qc3+ and QxN)} 28. Qc3+ Kg8 29. Qg3 {picks up the loose knight}) 26... Rxa3 $1 27. h4 ({I confess that I sat staring at the position after} 27. bxa3 {for some time before it dawned on me that Black simply plays} Qh4 $1 {and White cannot counter the threat of Qf6+. The Black queen has been out of play for so long that it is easy to forget about it}) 27... Nb3+ 28. Kb1 Ncd2+ 29. Kc2 Nd4+ 30. Kxd2 Nxe2 31. bxa3 Ng3 {Sources: BCM, February 1967, p37; CHESS, February 1967, Vol.32/521-2, p134; The Times, 4 January 1967 (notes are not in either magazine)} 0-1

