{Meanwhile, in the top game, Basman had managed to hang on, but was still under pressure (with 5 minutes for 11 moves) when he grabbed a hot pawn in the following position:} 35... Nxg4 36. Bxe6 {Seen by the spectators but not by Basman.} Nxf2 $1 ({Some desperado! Some spectators had been whispering "} 36... Qd4 {" but this would have been refuted by} 37. Qxf7+ Kh8 38. Qg8+ $1 Rxg8 39. Rxd4 {and White has won material.}) 37. Rf1 (37. Bxc8 $2 {would not win after} Nxd1 38. Qd8+ Qf8 39. Qxd1 $2 (39. Qxf8+ Kxf8 40. Bxb7 Nc3) 39... Qxc8) (37. Kxf2 {is refuted by} fxe6 {and if} ({Stockfish 17:} 37... Qb2+ $1 $17) 38. Qxe6+ Qf7+ {check!}) ({Finally, if} 37. Rd7 $4 Rc1+ {, etc.}) 37... Rf8 38. Bb3 Ng4 39. Qe4 Nf6 ({Stockfish 17:} 39... Nh6 $1 $17 {and if} 40. Qxb7 $2 Qd4+ 41. Kh1 Nf5 $19) 40. Qxb7 a5 41. Qf3 Nd7 42. Qd5 Nb6 43. Qxa5 {White has some pressure still but Black is over the worst.} Qd4+ 44. Rf2 Kg7 45. Qd2 Qc5 46. Qb2+ f6 47. Qc2 Rc8 48. Qxc5 Rxc5 49. Rd2 Rc6 {(the printed score is 'K-B2' here which is illegal. The move I have substituted for it seems to be the only one that doesn't necessitate any further score alterations - JS)} 50. Rd8 f5 51. Rb8 Kf6 52. a4 Nd7 {Now came tragedy. After refusing an offered draw, Littlewood goes chess-blind.} 53. a5 $4 Nxb8 {Source: CHESS, July 1968, Vol.33/559-60, p332 (comments by BN Green)} 0-1

