1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. d4 d6 4. c4 Nb6 5. f4 Bf5 6. Be3 e6 7. Nc3 g5 (7... dxe5 {is more usual but Basman has his own particular recipe in almost every opening; some move not quite 100 per cent sound but guaranteed to jolt his opponent into a slightly nervous state of mind and make him waste time on his clock.}) 8. fxg5 dxe5 9. dxe5 Nc6 10. Nf3 Nb4 11. Nd4 Be7 12. a3 Qxd4 $1 {Here is a typical Basman sacrifice. He coasts along to the brink of a positional precipice, then there is an atomic explosion and his opponent’s pieces are flying through the air as violently as his own.} ({Obviously} 12... Na6 13. Nxf5) ({or} 12... Nc6 13. Nxc6 {would leave Black's pawn structure disastrously shattered.}) 13. Bxd4 Nc2+ 14. Kf2 (14. Qxc2 Bxc2 15. Be3 {would leave White a pawn up (but who would play 14 Qxc2...?)}) 14... O-O-O 15. Bxb6 axb6 16. Qc1 ({And now} 16. Qxd8+ Rxd8 17. Rd1 {would have left White ahead in material...}) 16... h6 17. g4 Bh7 18. h4 hxg5 19. h5 Bc5+ {Basman is not the player to give up a finely-placed knight for a weakly-placed rook. Incidentally for his next 20 moves, Levy had left himself with barely three minutes - see note to Black’s 7th move.} 20. Kf3 f5 21. gxf5 Bxf5 22. Rh2 Nd4+ 23. Kg3 Nb3 24. Qe1 Rd4 25. Be2 Rd2 26. Rd1 Rxb2 27. Bf3 Rxh2 28. Kxh2 g4 29. Bg2 (29. Be4 {would have made things much harder for Black. Now White’s game sags.}) 29... Rxh5+ 30. Kg3 {An unfortunate square. The king would have been happier, after 29 Be4, on g2.} Be7 31. Qh1 {He must either return the queen or accept perpetual check by ...Bh4+ and ...Bg5+, etc.} Rxh1 32. Rxh1 Bxa3 {With three pawns and a bishop for rook, Black must win now.} 33. Rh8+ Kd7 34. Bxb7 Bb2 {The last crunch. If the attacked knight moves, 35...Bxe5+ wins the rook.} 35. Bc8+ Ke7 36. Rh1 Bxc3 37. Kf4 Nc5 38. Rd1 Nd3+ {Source: Illustrated London News, 12 October 1968 (BH Wood column)} (38... Nd3+ {After} 39. Ke3 Nxe5 {, he can't even play} 40. Rd7+) 0-1

