1. e4 e5 {(The original scoresheet missed out the next two half-moves and jumped straight to ...d5. I am grateful to Paul Cooksey for figuring out what was wrong with the score. Another possibility, pointed out by David McAlister, is that the game opened 1 f4 e5 2 e4, etc. The former is more likely as Basman's previous nine white games with 1 e4 e5 had continued 2 f4, while he did not take up with 1 f4 until August 1973)} 2. f4 d5 3. exd5 e4 4. d3 Nf6 5. Qe2 Bg4 6. Nf3 Qxd5 7. Nbd2 Bf5 8. dxe4 Nxe4 9. Ng5 Nc6 10. Ngxe4 O-O-O 11. Nc3 Qd7 12. Qb5 Qe6+ 13. Kf2 Nd4 14. Qe5 Qxe5 15. fxe5 Nxc2 16. Nb3 Nxa1 17. Nxa1 Bc5+ 18. Kf3 Rhe8 19. Nb3 Bb6 20. Bf4 Bd7 21. Be2 Bc6+ 22. Kg3 a5 23. a4 h6 24. h4 Kb8 25. Rd1 Rxd1 26. Bxd1 Rd8 27. Be2 Re8 28. Bc4 Re7 29. Nd5 Bxd5 30. Bxd5 Rd7 31. Bc4 Ka7 32. Kf3 Re7 33. h5 c6 34. Nc1 Bd4 35. Nd3 Ka8 36. Ke4 Bb6 37. Kf5 Bc7 38. g4 Kb8 39. b3 Kc8 40. g5 hxg5 41. Bxg5 Rd7 42. e6 Rd4 43. exf7 {Source: Basman scoresheet (https://michael-basman-legacy.github.io/scoresheets/1973/Birmingham%20Open%2010-3-73.pdf)} 1-0

