1. b3 e5 2. Bb2 Nc6 3. e3 d5 4. Nf3 e4 5. Nd4 Nxd4 6. Bxd4 Nf6 7. c4 c5 $2 {Black hopes for 5 Bb2 when 8...d4 is quite strong. But White's reply weakens Black's central position.} 8. Bxf6 Qxf6 9. Nc3 {Black's e and d-pawns are now weak; it will be hard to defend them without losing time.} dxc4 (9... d4 10. Nxe4 Qe5 11. Bd3 Bf5 {was the alternative.}) 10. Bxc4 Qg6 11. Qc2 ({Black is willing to forego castling, in the hope that his king can escape to the q-side, e.g. if now} 11. Bb5+ Bd7 12. Bxd7+ Kxd7 {followed by ...Rd8 and ...Kc8-b8. So White decides instead on quick development.}) 11... Qxg2 {Might as well be hung for a sheep as for a lamb.} 12. O-O-O Bd7 13. Rhg1 Qf3 14. Be2 {White must play accurately if he wants to cash in on Black's troubles.} Qf5 $2 ({Black wants to retain his e-pawn, but the best chance was to play} 14... Qf6 15. Qxe4+ Qe6 ({or} 15... Be7 16. Qxb7 Rd8 {once again coming out with fewer pawns}) 16. Qxb7 Qc6 17. Qxd7+ Kxd7 18. Bb5 {going into an endgame a pawn down. Now the roof falls in.}) 15. Bg4 Qe5 16. Bxd7+ Kxd7 {Lining him up for the knock-out blow...} 17. d4 {More effective than if it were played at move one.} exd3 18. Qxd3+ {Sources: BCM, May 1976, p212; StarBase 4.56 (which has the wrong move order 3...Nf6/6...d5); Basman scoresheet (https://michael-basman-legacy.github.io/scoresheets/1975/YMCA%20Elo%2020-9-75.pdf)} (18. Qxd3+ Bd6 19. Nb5 {wins a piece.}) 1-0

