1. d4 c6 2. Nf3 d5 3. c4 dxc4 4. e3 Be6 $6 {Typical Basman - I decided now to forget about regaining the pawn.} ({4 e3 is better but} 4... Nf6 {may not be so bad.}) 5. Nc3 Qa5 6. Bd2 Nd7 7. Be2 Ngf6 8. O-O h6 9. e4 Qc7 10. Rc1 Rc8 11. b3 $5 cxb3 12. axb3 (12. d5 $1 {is better, but after} b2 $1 13. dxe6 bxc1=Q 14. exd7+ {Black has R+2Ps for two pieces and I wanted more.}) 12... Qb8 13. d5 $6 (13. h3 $5) 13... Bg4 $1 (13... cxd5 14. exd5 Nxd5 15. Nxd5 Bxd5 16. Bf4 $1 {gives a winning attack.}) 14. g3 $5 g5 {Stopping Bf4.} 15. Nd4 Bh3 16. Re1 Ne5 {Maybe  already - White has not enough for a pawn.} 17. f4 gxf4 18. Bxf4 Rg8 19. Qd2 h5 $6 (19... Bg7 {or even 19...e6 might be better.}) 20. Nf3 Nfg4 21. Nxe5 (21. Ng5 $5 Rxg5 22. Bxg5 b5 $13) 21... Nxe5 22. Bxh5 Bg7 23. Kh1 Bf6 24. Qe2 Qd6 ({I expected} 24... Rh8 {when perhaps} 25. g4 {is good.}) 25. Bxf7+ {Winning a pawn - but in dangerous circumstances.} Kxf7 26. Qh5+ Rg6 27. Qxh3 Rh8 28. Qf5 Nf3 $1 29. Re2 (29. Bxd6 $2 Rxh2#) 29... Qa3 $2 (29... Qc5 $1 {is very dangerous:} 30. e5 Nd4 31. Qe4 Nxe2 32. Qxe2 {with about equal chances is probably best.}) 30. Rf2 $1 Rxg3 {The point of Black's play - but it loses!} 31. Rcf1 $1 {Avoiding the threat of Qxc1+ and Rg1 mate.} Nd4 32. Qd7 {Suddenly White's attack is winning.} Rxc3 33. Be5 $1 Rf3 34. Bxf6 Kxf6 35. Rxf3+ Nxf3 36. Qf5+ $1 (36. Rxf3+ $2 Ke5 {is not so clear.}) 36... Kg7 37. Qxf3 Qd6 38. Rg1+ {Source: Hartston's notes in the bulletin; CHESS, October 1977, Vol.43 Nos.779-780, p17} 1-0

