1. e4 g6 2. Nc3 Bg7 3. a3 $3 {A stupendous waiting move. Since Black is being so cagey about his formation, why should White commit himself either? The move a3 is not without point as will be seen in the further course of the opening.} d6 4. Bc4 {Now Black has played d6, White can bring his bishop out on this diagonal, as to shut it out by ...c6 and ...d5 will result in a small loss of tempo (the d-pawn has moved twice). Also, the move a3 allocates a square at a2 for the white bishop should it in the subsequent course of the game be attacked by pawns and knights. In fact it is very tempting for Black to try a 'blot out' formation with pawns at c6, d5 and e6 - quite feasible, but hardly a normal Pirc formation, and therefore the territory becomes unfam­iliar to the ordinary Pirc player.} Nf6 5. d3 ({White's game is taking shape. He does not make the 'blunder'} 5. d4 {which allows} Nxe4 6. Nxe4 d5 {(see count­less Basman-Nunn games) but strengthens his outposted pawn at e4. Both white bishops now have good diagonals, and White has at his disposal a fair choice of plans - his f-pawn is not blocked (by Nf3) so he may choose to play f4-f5 later on. He may also consider castling on either side.}) 5... O-O 6. Bg5 c6 7. Qd2 ({The order and choice of moves at this stage of the game is quite important, as Black is getting ready to make his thrust ...d5. In order to prevent this White could have played} 7. Nge2 {. Then if} d5 8. exd5 cxd5 9. Bxf6 Bxf6 {White can simply win a pawn by} 10. Bxd5 {since} Bxc3+ {is answered by} 11. Nxc3 {recapturing and guarding the bishop at d5.}) 7... Nbd7 ({In the game continuation after 7 Qd2, Black could here have played} 7... d5 {since after} 8. exd5 cxd5 9. Bxf6 Bxf6 10. Bxd5 {is not possible (because of 10...Bxc3 and 11...Qxd5)} ({so White would have to capture with the knight -} 10. Nxd5 {. Black would play} Bxb2 11. Rb1 Bxa3 {and despite White's active-looking position, I haven't found a convincing continuation for him.})) 8. Nge2 b5 {Black goes over to another plan of the 'fluid' PIRC - the queen's side pawn ava­lanche. Note he does not wish to place his pawns on c5 or e5 yet, because that would increase the scope of White's light-squared bishop.} 9. Ba2 a5 10. b4 Bb7 ({White's third move (a3) has also enabled him to stop the queen's side advance dead in its tracks. Now if Black plays} 10... axb4 11. axb4 c5 {, after} 12. Rb1 {he is liable to be saddled with a very weak b-pawn. He now endeavours to make something of the a-file, without much success.}) 11. O-O Ra6 12. Rab1 Qa8 13. Bb3 {White gets his pieces out of the way just in time and whether Black now plays 13...axb4 or 13...a4 14 Ba2, there's nothing for him on the a-file.} e6 {A significant advance. If Black follows this up with ...d5, he gains by smothering the white bishop on b3, but his own bishop at b7 is no better off. Meanwhile, White can begin a king's side assault with f4-f5.} 14. Rfe1 Re8 15. Bh6 Bh8 16. h3 Qd8 17. Ng3 Ra8 18. Kh1 axb4 19. axb4 {You may have noticed a slight hesitancy in White's movements at this point. He is trying to favourably engineer the pawn advance f4-f5, since the initiative has now passed into his hands. However, he has not gone about it the right way and allows Black a bit of tactics.} Ng4 $1 20. hxg4 Bxc3 21. Qxc3 Qh4+ 22. Kg1 Qxh6 23. Qd4 Qf8 ({Had Black seen the strength of White's next move he would probably have chosen} 23... Qf4) 24. e5 $1 d5 ({Now Black is in trouble again; he cannot play 24...dxe5 because of 25 Qxd7. If he takes with the knight, White moves to Ne4 threatening Nf6+, and the complications go against Black. For example, if} 24... Nxe5 25. Ne4 Nxg4 ({or if} 25... Qg7 {, guarding f6 and threatening 26...Nf3+ winning White's queen, White's strongest reply is} 26. Qb6 {, capturing the d-pawn next move with his knight}) 26. f3 f5 27. Nxd6) {Black reluctantly accepts this pawn formation, reminiscent of the French defence with an extremely bad bishop at b7. As White has firm control of d4, endgame and middle game positions are liable to be very good for him.} 25. g5 c5 26. bxc5 Nxc5 27. Nf1 {It was also possible to put this knight on e2, in order to occupy the d4 square later, but White was trying to make capital out of the weak square in Black's king's side by the manoeuvre Nf1-e3-g4-f6+.} Qe7 28. Ne3 (28. f4 {maintains the bind, but White thinks he has seen a forced win ...so he does not bother to guard the g5-pawn.}) 28... Nxb3 {Demise of a bishop which, while not appearing to have been very active, has in fact dictated the whole course of the game.} 29. cxb3 Qxg5 {Accepting the bait.} 30. Ng4 {Threatening to win the exchange by Nf6+.} Rec8 31. Nf6+ Kg7 32. f4 Qh4 33. Re2 ({All according to plan; but unfortunately White now realises that the "winning" move} 33. Re3 {(threatening 34 Rh3 trapping the black queen) actually loses after} Ra2 $1 34. Rh3 Rxg2+ $3 35. Kxg2 Rc2+ {. So precautions are first neces­sary to guard the second rank.}) 33... Rc6 34. Rf1 Rac8 {Black masses down the c-file ready to play 35...Rc1, after which he will be on top. So White must get in first.} 35. g3 Qxg3+ 36. Rg2 Qh3 37. Rh2 Qg3+ 38. Kh1 ({Declining the draw by} 38. Rg2 {, White maintains his attack on the h-pawn and also has a second threat...}) 38... Rc1 ({... : if say Black defends with} 38... h6 39. Nh5+ $1 gxh5 40. Rg2 {winning the black queen.}) 39. Rxh7+ Kf8 40. Qb4+ R8c5 $1 41. Rh8+ Kg7 ({Not of course} 41... Ke7 42. Re8#) 42. Rh7+ Kf8 {Source: Rabbits Review no.1 (1978), ppn 17-23. Contributed by Andy Ansel, 16 November 2025. (I can find no confirmation of the date of this event - perhaps it was earlier than 1978? JS)} (42... Kf8 {Here a draw was agreed. White can do no better than perpetual here, since if he tries} 43. Rxc1 $2 d4+ 44. Ne4 Bxe4+ 45. dxe4 Qf3+ 46. Kg1 Qe3+ 47. Kg2 Qxc1 {is even in Black's favour. A good save by Black who thus made sure of first prize in the tournament but as for the opening...}) 1/2-1/2

