1. e4 b6 2. d4 Bb7 3. Nd2 e6 4. Ngf3 Nf6 5. Bd3 c5 6. c3 Qc7 7. O-O Be7 8. Re1 O-O 9. Nf1 d6 10. Bg5 Nbd7 11. e5 $5 ({Of course, White can get an excellent game by a slow build-up such as} 11. Ng3 {. But the text move was interesting and seemed to give Black some immediate problems to solve. 11 Ng3 may yet be best.}) 11... dxe5 12. dxe5 Nd5 $2 ({The losing move.} 12... Bxf3 13. Qxf3 Nxe5 14. Qg3 Bd6 15. Bxf6 Ng6 16. Qf3 gxf6 17. Qxf6 {, when White has a small but clear edge, is better.}) 13. c4 Bxg5 14. Nxg5 Nf4 15. Bxh7+ Kh8 16. g3 $1 {Black has no answer to this simple move.} (16. Qg4 Nxe5 $1 17. Qh4 Nxg2 18. Qh3 Nf4) (16. Be4 Nxe5) 16... f6 17. gxf4 fxg5 18. Qh5 Rxf4 19. Bf5+ Kg8 20. Bxe6+ Kf8 21. Qh8+ Ke7 22. Qxg7+ Kxe6 (22... Ke8 23. Qg6+ Kf8 24. Qg8+) 23. Qg6+ Ke7 24. Qxg5+ Ke6 25. Qxf4 Rf8 26. Qh6+ Ke7 27. Rad1 {Source: CHESS, May 1978, p247} 1-0

