{White plays the opening haphazardly but some underlying choices trouble Black. He allows White to launch a sacrificial attack - which works! On move 25 Black is two pawns down and could resign. Now read on...} 1. e4 e6 2. Nc3 a6 3. Nf3 b5 4. d4 Bb7 5. Bd3 c5 6. dxc5 Bxc5 7. a3 Qc7 8. O-O Nc6 ({Better} 8... Ne7 {immediately.}) 9. b4 Ba7 10. Bb2 Nge7 11. Bxb5 axb5 12. Nxb5 Qb6 (12... Qb8 {possibly better.}) 13. Nd6+ Kf8 14. b5 Bc8 15. Rb1 Nd8 16. Bd4 Qa5 17. b6 Ba6 18. Re1 Bb8 19. b7 Nxb7 20. Nxb7 Qh5 21. Nc5 Bc8 22. Nxd7+ Bxd7 23. Ne5 {Beautiful! White's combin­ative play inspires applause.} Qxd1 24. Nxd7+ Kg8 25. Rexd1 Bd6 {No doubt White could win simply by pushing his passed a-pawn. But he decides to finish off in style.} 26. Nb6 Re8 27. Nc4 Bb8 28. Na5 h5 29. Bc5 Ng6 30. Rb7 Rc8 31. Nb3 h4 32. g3 Ne5 33. f4 Ng4 {Black has some play though White is still winning if he plays accurately.} 34. Rdd7 hxg3 35. hxg3 Rh3 36. Rd3 ({Bluff. White should play} 36. Kg2 Rh2+ 37. Kf3 f5 38. Rxg7+ Kh8 39. Rxg4 {here.} ({Stockfish 17:} 39. exf5 exf5 40. Rgf7 $18)) 36... g5 $1 {The bishop at b8 becomes active. White now loses through demoralisation at the dis­appearance of his won position.} 37. Rbd7 Kg7 38. Nd4 Kg6 39. f5+ exf5 40. exf5+ Kg7 {Reaching the time control at move 40.} 41. Be7 $4 ({White could still draw now by the combination} 41. Ne6+ Kf6 42. Rxf7+ Kxf7 43. Nxg5+ Kg8 44. Nxh3 Rxc5 {elimi­nating all Black's pawns.}) 41... Ne5 42. Kg2 g4 43. Rb7 Nxd3 44. cxd3 Rxg3+ 45. Kf2 Rxd3 46. Ne2 Rf3+ 47. Ke1 Rxf5 48. Rb4 g3 49. Rg4+ Kh6 50. Bb4 Re8 51. Kd1 Rf1+ {A last-round swizz! Source: St George's Defence by MJ Basman (Audio Chess)} 0-1

