{A disaster. Could it ever have happened with the black pieces?} 1. b4 e5 2. a3 d5 3. e3 a5 4. Bb2 Nd7 5. c4 ({Simpler is} 5. b5 c5 6. c4 d4 7. e4 {with equality.}) 5... axb4 6. Nf3 $5 ({Black disregards his d-pawn in the belief that he can pick it up later; now White could play} 6. axb4 Rxa1 7. Bxa1 Bxb4 8. Qa4 ({or more sharply} 8. f4) 8... Bd6 9. Nf3 Ngf6 $1) 6... e4 7. Nd4 Ngf6 8. axb4 ({Possible here is} 8. Be2 {followed by 0-0 and f3.}) 8... Rxa1 9. Bxa1 Bxb4 10. Qb3 {White now regains his pawn but runs into trouble defending it.} Bd6 11. cxd5 O-O 12. Bc4 Nc5 13. Qa2 Ng4 $1 {Very dangerous.} 14. Nc3 ({White must play} 14. h4 {here, and if} Qf6 {,} 15. f3 {. But his game is difficult.}) 14... Qh4 15. g3 Qh3 16. Nce2 Qg2 17. Rf1 Nd3+ {Source: St George's Defence by MJ Basman (Audio Chess)} 0-1

