{Round 10 was the big match, Black against Yehuda Gruenfeld, Interzonal qualifier and one point ahead of the rest of the field and 1.5 points ahead of me. Gruenfeld always played his moves quickly and as the tournament progressed seemed to be playing faster than ever. He disposed of Czerniak in 20 moves and an hour's play. I decided to take my time as, how­ever quick sight a player has of the board, he is nonetheless liable to overlook things if he plays too fast. From the opening I emerged satisfactorily but then White's fast manoeuvring began to make up ground.} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 a6 3. Nf3 b5 4. a4 Bb7 5. Nbd2 b4 6. Bd3 c5 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. Qe2 Ne7 9. Nb3 Ba7 10. Be3 Bxe3 11. Qxe3 d6 ({This should have dropped a pawn to 12 Qd4, so better might have been} 11... O-O) 12. O-O O-O 13. Rad1 Qc7 14. Rd2 Nd7 15. Rfd1 Rfd8 16. Qg5 Nc6 17. Be2 Nf6 18. Qf4 e5 19. Qe3 Ne7 20. Bd3 Ng6 21. g3 h6 22. Ne1 Rac8 23. Qe2 Qb6 24. Ng2 {[Position in CHESS.] Here White was threatening to take the initiative with the man­oeuvre 25 Ne3-c4, so I knew I had to act fast to stay in the game.} d5 $1 25. exd5 e4 26. Bxe4 Re8 {The point. White is unable to defend his bishop by 27 f3 as this pawn is pinned. Of course White can defend in other ways.} 27. Rd4 Bxd5 {Regaining one of the pawns.} 28. Rxd5 ({A quick play error.} 28. a5 {first would have been slightly better.}) 28... Rxe4 29. Qd3 Ne5 30. Rd6 ({[I had been] hoping that White might fall for} 30. Rd8+ Kh7 31. Qd6 Nf3+ 32. Kf1 Ng4 $1 33. Qxb6 Ngxh2# {but I don't supposed he considered it.}) 30... Nxd3 31. Rxb6 Nxb2 {Black has the advantage in this endgame but with careful play White can draw.} 32. Ra1 Rxc2 33. Ne3 $1 Rc3 34. Nd2 Re8 35. Rxb4 Nd3 36. Rb3 Rxb3 37. Nxb3 Ne4 38. Nd1 Rc8 39. Kg2 $1 Rc2 40. Nd4 Rc4 41. Nf3 a5 {Up to now Gruenfeld had firmly resisted all Black's pressure, but now he slipped up with} 42. Ra3 $2 Ndxf2 $1 {- a sacrifice which would not have been possible with the rook at a1, ready to move over to f1.} 43. Nxf2 Rc2 44. Re3 $2 ({Stockfish 17:} 44. Nd4 $11) 44... Nxf2 45. Nd4 Ra2 {Here White even dropped an­other pawn after} 46. Re2 $2 Rxa4 47. Nf5 (47. Rxf2 Rxd4) 47... Ne4 48. h4 h5 49. Kf3 g6 50. Ne7+ Kf8 51. Nc6 Nd6 52. Re3 Nf5 53. Rd3 Rg4 54. Kf2 a4 55. Ne5 Re4 56. Nd7+ Kg7 57. Nb6 Rb4 58. Nd5 Rb3 59. Rxb3 axb3 60. Kf3 b2 61. Nc3 Kf6 62. Kf4 Ke6 63. Nb1 Nd4 64. g4 hxg4 65. Kxg4 f5+ 66. Kg5 Kf7 67. Kf4 Kf6 68. Ke3 Ke5 69. Na3 Nc2+ {Sources: Basman/Welling Collection; CHESS, October 1979, Vol.44/825-6, p381} 0-1

