{At the beginning of the 11th and last round the scores were Gruenfeld 8.5/2, Jokstic [sic] 8, Basman 7.5. I was paired with the Yugoslav Jokstic [sic] and I saw him chatting to his compatriot Jlijc [sic] before the game began so I knew what I was in for. At first the game seemed to be going well for Black.} 1. g4 d5 2. h3 g6 3. d3 Bg7 4. Bg2 c5 5. Nc3 e6 6. Bf4 Nc6 7. Qd2 a6 8. a4 h6 9. Bg3 {White is shaping to expand in the centre with e4 and f4. Black takes the opportunity to castle.} Nge7 10. e4 O-O 11. f4 dxe4 12. Nxe4 {White is unwilling to exchange queens yet, preferring to sacrifice his b-pawn instead.} Bxb2 13. Rb1 Bg7 14. Ne2 c4 $5 {Rather than defend his c-pawn with ...b6 Black gives it up in order to force the exchange of queens and isolate all of White's queen's side pawns.} 15. dxc4 Qxd2+ 16. Nxd2 Na5 17. Bf2 $1 {After this move it becomes ap­parent that Black is unable to exploit the doubled c-pawns and in fact gets into a stranglehold on the queen's side.} e5 18. Bb6 Nec6 19. O-O exf4 {At this point in the game Hans Suri, the tournament organizer, called me down and took a photo­graph of me standing in front of a bicycle.} 20. Rxf4 {White captures with the rook rather than allow Black freedom to play 20...Bd4+ exchang­ing bishops, which would have been the reply to 20 Nxf4.} Re8 21. Rf2 f5 $5 {Aggressive play. Jokstic [sic] now left the table, had a look at Gruenfeld's game (he was losing to Titz) and came back to offer me a draw.} 22. Bd5+ Kh7 {White's bishops dominate yet Black's game proves very hard to breach.} 23. Nf4 fxg4 24. Bf7 Rf8 25. Bxg6+ Kg8 26. hxg4 Bxg4 27. Be4 Bd4 {At last Black is able to exchange the bishops.} 28. Bd5+ Kg7 29. Bxd4+ Nxd4 30. Kh2 h5 31. c3 {I had just decided that 31...Ne2 was going to level the chances, when Black came out with the colossal error} Nf3+ $4 {This made me happy after} 32. Rxf3 Bxf3 33. Ne6+ Kf6 34. Nxf3 Rfe8 35. Nfg5 Rac8 {[#]Can you work out the winning sequence from here?} 36. Rf1+ Ke7 37. Rf7+ Kd6 38. Ne4+ Ke5 39. Nd4 Rf8 40. Nf3# {Sources: CHESS, October 1979, Vol.44/825-6, p382; Macho Grob by Mike Basman (Audio Chess) (in the booklet MJB refers to Black as 'Jockstick'!)} 1-0

