1. g4 d5 2. h3 e5 3. d3 c5 4. Bg2 Nc6 5. c4 d4 6. Nf3 (6. Nd2 Be7 7. Bxc6+ bxc6 8. Qa4 Qc7 9. Ngf3 Bd7 10. Nb3 Nf6 11. Qa5 Qxa5+ 12. Nxa5 {Basman-Davies, City Chess Quickplay 1989}) 6... h5 7. g5 Nge7 8. Nbd2 Ng6 9. Ne4 Be6 10. h4 Qd7 11. a3 {White has quite a good position, but it will be difficult to effect the advance b4. He could have considered moving his Ns around with Ng3, Nf3-d2, and Be4 and Nf3 again. Black must also be careful not to do too much in this position - for example, he'd better not castle.} a5 12. Bd2 (12. Rb1 b6 13. Bd2 {is the move order given at chessgames.com}) 12... b6 13. Rb1 Rb8 14. Qa4 Be7 15. Ng1 {Threatening Nf6+ and Bxc6.} Na7 16. Qc2 Nc6 17. Ng3 {Preparing to transfer the bishop to e4.} Nf4 18. Be4 Bg4 $1 ({Black defends well; he needs a retreat square for his N on f4, so} 18... g6 {will not do.}) 19. Nf3 ({Probably better is} 19. Qa4 {straightaway; as played Black gets a N to e7 and this attacks the important f5 square.}) 19... Bd6 {White was threatening 20 Nxe5 and 21 Bxf4.} 20. Qa4 Ne7 21. Qxd7+ Kxd7 22. Nh2 Be6 23. b4 axb4 24. axb4 f5 25. gxf6 gxf6 26. Bf3 cxb4 27. Ne4 Neg6 28. Nf1 Be7 29. Bxb4 Bg4 30. Nfd2 {Black has made progress; he should now have continued 30...f5 - but on the last move before the time-control he blunders.} Bh3 {Played to surr­ound the h-pawn, this almost loses a piece.} 31. Bxe7 Kxe7 32. e3 Bg2 $1 ({Black can save his piece by} 32... Nxd3+ 33. Ke2 Be6 34. Kxd3 f5 {but after} 35. exd4 {, his position is not good. So he gives up two pieces for a pawn and a rook.}) 33. exf4 Bxh1 34. Bxh1 Nxf4 35. Ng3 {White cedes another pawn rather than allow Black's rooks along the a-file.} Nxd3+ 36. Kf1 {Black’s pawns are not danger­ous, but it is difficult for White to make progress.} Nf4 37. Be4 Rhd8 38. Ne2 Ne6 39. Bg6 Rh8 40. Ra1 Ra8 41. Rb1 Rab8 42. Bf5 Ng7 43. Be4 Ne6 44. Bd5 {White is manoeuvring either to be able to play N-g3-f5 without allowing ...Ke6, or R-al-a7 in favourable circumstances. With the second time-control app­roaching Black loses patience.} f5 45. f4 d3 {I missed this but fortunately there is a reply.} 46. Ng3 Nxf4 47. Nxf5+ Kf6 48. Ne3 Rhf8 49. Ne4+ Kg7 50. Ke1 {Here the game was adjourned. If White can get his king to d2 Black’s game is hopeless. His pawns are weak, and worse White will begin an attack with Rgl+.} Nxd5 51. cxd5 $1 ({This gives White a passed pawn though it lets a rook in on f4. The alternative} 51. Nxd5 {is less good after} Rbc8 52. Nxb6 Rc6 {and White's N's are a bit tangled up.}) 51... Rf4 52. Nc5 $1 {The N threatens to come in on e6, d7, or even capture on d3.} Kh7 $1 ({The best chance although the king gets a little further from the d-pawn. If} 52... Rxh4 53. Nf5+ {forks king and rook.}) 53. Ne6 $1 {Controls the d8 queening square and d4 so that both Black's rooks are prevented from stopping the passed d-pawn.} ({The move order shown is that given in Basman's BCM annotation.} 53. Kd2 Rxh4 54. Ne6 {is the move order given in Basman's Killer Grob book - JS}) 53... Rxh4 {Another pawn gone - now Black only needs to exchange a pair of rooks and capture the d-pawn to make a drawn two Ns v K ending.} 54. Kd2 e4 55. d6 Rc8 $1 56. Nf5 ({Against} 56. d7 $2 Rh2+ {pins the white king to the back rank.}) 56... Rh2+ 57. Ke3 Re2+ 58. Kf4 d2 59. d7 Ra8 ({If} 59... Rg8 {, not 60 Rh1 Rh2!, but} 60. Ng5+ {. White now thought for 20 minutes before playing the winning combination.}) 60. Rg1 $1 {Everything is now forced.} ({In fact, 60 Rg1? throws away the forced win. Stockfish 17:} 60. Ng5+ $1 Kg8 (60... Kg6 61. Rxb6#) (60... Kh8 61. Rg1 $1 $18) 61. Ne7+ Kh8 62. Rh1 Rh2 63. Nc8 $1 $18) 60... Re1 61. Rg7+ Kh8 62. Ne7 $1 Rf1+ 63. Ke5 $1 ({Not} 63. Ke3 Rf6 $1 {and White is in trouble.}) 63... Ra5+ 64. Kxe4 Re1+ 65. Kf4 Rf1+ ({Stockfish 17:} 65... Ra4+ $1 66. Kf5 Ra5+ 67. Kf6 Rxe6+ $1 68. Kxe6 d1=Q 69. Rg8+ Kh7 70. d8=Q $11) 66. Ke3 Ra3+ (66... d1=N+ {is no good after} 67. Ke2) 67. Kxd2 {Sources: bulletin (with notes by Basman); BCM, November 1979, p527 (also with notes by Basman); 'The Killer Grob' by Michael Basman (Pergamon) (Stockfish 17 assessments added by John Saunders)} 1-0

