1. g4 e5 2. h3 d5 3. Bg2 Ne7 4. c4 d4 5. Qb3 c6 6. Qg3 {The bizarre opening pawn advance is a favourite of England international Basman. Here its justifica­tion is a still more unorthodox queen development.} Ng6 7. d3 Bd6 8. Be4 Nd7 ({More active is} 8... Qh4) 9. Nf3 Qc7 10. Nbd2 Nf6 11. a3 a5 12. b3 Bd7 13. e3 c5 14. g5 Nxe4 15. Nxe4 Bc6 16. h4 O-O-O ({If} 16... h5 {to stop White gaining space then} 17. gxh6 Rxh6 18. Qg4) 17. h5 Ne7 18. Nxd6+ Qxd6 19. e4 f6 20. Bd2 b6 21. b4 Qc7 22. bxa5 bxa5 23. h6 Rhg8 24. Qh2 Ng6 25. hxg7 f5 26. exf5 Bxf3 27. fxg6 Bxh1 28. gxh7 $1 Qb7 29. h8=Q Bf3 30. Q8h3+ Kb8 31. Qxe5+ Ka8 32. Qxc5 {Remarkable white strategy, though Black's whole approach was too passive. Source: The Field, 7 April 1982} 1-0

