1. e4 g5 {Mike Basman played the Grob throughout the tournament, scoring 5.5/6 to this point. One opponent played 1 Nf3, to which the reply was 1..h6 and 2... g5!} 2. d4 h6 3. c3 $1 d6 $1 (3... Bg7 $2 4. h4 $1 gxh4 5. Qg4 $16) 4. Bd3 Nf6 5. Ne2 Rg8 $5 6. O-O h5 7. f4 g4 $8 8. e5 Nd5 9. c4 Nb4 10. Bh7 Rh8 11. Be4 c6 12. a3 N4a6 13. Nbc3 Nc7 14. Bd3 $1 d5 $6 {To counter White's 'slight' development advantage (the consequence of playing Alekhine's Defence as well as the Grob!) by blocking the centre, but now White has a very strong attack.} 15. f5 Bg7 16. Ng3 $1 (16. Nf4 dxc4 {Bxf5}) 16... Nd7 $8 {Otherwise 17 Nxh5 is winning} 17. f6 exf6 18. Nf5 (18. Nxh5 $2 Rxh5 19. Qxg4 Rg5) 18... Bf8 19. Nd6+ Bxd6 20. exd6 Ne6 21. cxd5 Nxd4 22. dxc6 $6 bxc6 23. Bc4 Ne6 {White gave Black this good square for the knight. Now 24 Kh1 would allow 24...Ne5} 24. Ne4 Ba6 25. Bxa6 Qb6+ 26. Kh1 Qxa6 27. Nxf6+ Nxf6 28. Rxf6 Rd8 (28... O-O-O $2 29. Rxf7 $18) 29. Bf4 { Qb3} Qc4 $1 30. Qd2 h4 31. Rc1 Qd5 { 32...h3} 32. Qc3 $1 Rh5 $2 ({In mutual time shortage, Black goes astray. After} 32... Rh7 33. Qxc6+ Qxc6 34. Rxc6 Rh5 $1 {Black is a pawn down but still in the game.}) 33. Rxe6+ fxe6 (33... Qxe6 34. Re1) 34. Qg7 $18 Rxd6 35. Bxd6 Qg5 36. Qxg5 ({Quicker was} 36. Qf8+ {and 27 Rd1.}) 36... Rxg5 37. Rc5 Rg8 38. Kg1 Kd7 39. Be5 Rf8 40. Ra5 Ra8 41. Ra4 h3 42. Rxg4 c5 43. gxh3 Kc6 44. Bc3 {Sources: John Nicholson; Portsmouth Evening News, 15 April 1989} 1-0

