1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Bc4 Nf6 5. O-O Nxe4 6. Re1 ({White is two pawns behind and must act energetically to make his lead in development stick. Both 6.e2 and} 6. Nxd4 {are insufficiently powerful after Black replies} d5) 6... d5 7. Bxd5 ({The temporary sacrifice xd5 recovers White all his lost ground.} 7. Rxe4+ {is a wild attempt to win which is refuted after} dxe4 8. Ng5 Ne5 $1) 7... Qxd5 8. Nc3 ({Once again only the most accurate move will do. After} 8. c4 Qxc4 9. Rxe4+ Be6 10. Nxd4 O-O-O $1 {White has little to be proud of.}) (8. Ng5 Be7 (8... f5 $6 9. f3) 9. Nxe4 Be6 {regains the piece but not the pawn. 8.c3 is a brilliant piece of opening theory that immediately recovers the piece with gain of time.}) 8... Qd8 9. Rxe4+ ({We are following the game of Ragozin against Botvinnik, Leningrad 1930. Botvinnik's 8..d8 is not the most popular move ( more common are ..h5 or ..a5 ). However, on d8 the queen is at least out of the centre and still guards the extra pawn at d4. White must continue to play energetically as, if the initiative fizzles out, he may find that Black has the two bishops in an open position plus an extra pawn.} 9. Bg5 {would be met by} Be7 10. Nxe4 O-O $1 {and black has safely spirited his king to safety.}) (9. Nxe4 {recovers the piece and threatens f6++ ; Black will probably reply} Be7 10. Qd3 O-O {and White is a pawn behind. 9.xe4+ keeps up the pressure.}) 9... Be6 ({The stemgame, Ragozin-Botvinnik continued} 9... Be7 10. Nxd4 f5 11. Rf4 O-O 12. Nxc6 Qxd1+ 13. Nxd1 bxc6 14. Rc4 Bd6 {with an unclear position - but Black did not know this line and could think of no satistactory continuation after 9..e7 10.xd4, so he chose the inferior 9..e6.}) 10. Nxd4 Nxd4 11. Rxd4 Qc8 ({If} 11... Bd6 12. Ne4 {wins Whie at least a pawn}) ({Or if} 11... Qf6 12. Nb5) 12. Bg5 ({A fine attacking move, which really does threaten to win the queen with d8+.} 12. Nd5 {is quite strong, but it is better to bring another man into the fray rather tham attacking only with the men at your disposal.}) 12... f6 ({If Black plays} 12... Be7 {, White replies} 13. Bxe7 Kxe7 14. Qh5 Rd8 15. Rxd8 Qxd8 16. Rd1 {with a winning position.}) 13. Bxf6 {The best way to continue the attack, smashing the defensive pawn barrier in front of the black king. ]} gxf6 14. Qh5+ {[ At this point in the game Black surprisingly resigned ! -under a miscalculation. But we shall continue the game with a 'might have been ' series of moves. ]} Bf7 15. Re1+ ({The natural move , because it brings a piece into the action with gain of time. But} 15. Re4+ {is also strong - for example after} Be7 16. Qe2 {and Black will have to return his piece by 16..0-0 , since} Qd8 17. Rd1 {is unbearable.}) 15... Be7 16. Rxe7+ ({The move} 16. Qc5 {looks extremely powerful, but it is not, because Black can reply} Be6 $1) ({Likewise} 16. Qe2 {looks strong ( and it was because of this move that Black had resigned ! ) but he had overlooked} O-O 17. Qxe7 ({White can still gain the advantage if he remembers to play} 17. Qg4+ $1 Kh8 18. Rxe7 {when White is a pawn ahead.}) 17... Re8 $1 {and Black wins, because he has a skewer and a back row mate to turn the tables on White. Such is the treacherousness of the chess playing jungle - in one move a victory can turn into an ego-crushing defeat.}) 16... Kxe7 17. Qc5+ ({White must again choose his moves with caution because Black can easily escape against careless play ; and don't forget that Black is a rook ahead , even though those rooks are useless at the moment. 17.c5+ is the sort of check that prevents Black's king slipping to safety via f8. For example , against the weaker} 17. Re4+ Kf8 18. Qh6+ Kg8 19. Qxf6 h5 20. Re7 Qf8 {Black puts the defence together.}) 17... Ke8 18. Re4+ ({What of} 18. Nb5 {..? At first sight this is extremely attractive - it threatens xc7+ and - if Black defends with 18..c6? then 19.d6+ forks the king and queen. But something against the crudity and materialism of the move warns us against it and indeed Black does have a reasonable answer in} Be6 $1 19. Nxc7+ Kf7 {. Here} 20. Qh5+ {keeps the attack alive, but after} Kg7 {things are not as rosy as before - the king has done a runner. 18.e4+ , though, if followed up correctly, brings the best results.}) 18... Be6 19. Nd5 ({The winning blow which threatens 20.e7 mate and is decisive because, after the natural 20..d7, White wins the black queen by 20.xf6+. Other moves are less effective.} 19. Qd5 {is answered satisfactorily by} Kf7) 19... Kf7 20. Qe7+ Kg8 21. Nxf6# {Source: Mike Basman article. Contributor: Gerard Welling.} 1-0

