1. b4 d5 2. Bb2 Nf6 3. e3 Bf5 4. f4 e6 5. a3 c5 {Black is up for the fight, immediately contesting White's domination of the dark squares over the whole board.} 6. Nf3 $5 {A pawn sacrifice to accelerate development.} cxb4 7. Bb5+ Nc6 8. Ne5 Qb6 9. Qe2 Bxc2 {A fridge too far. Black gorges himself on another pawn, but inadvertently opens the c file, which leads to his demise.} 10. O-O $1 a6 $2 {Another over-optimistic move. Black does not want to move his king's bishop (although he needs to in order to castle), because then, after White plays axb4, he will have to move it again. But this attempt is doomed to failure, as White magically develops his queen's rook.} 11. axb4 $1 {Now Black cannot capture at b5, because of the pin along the a-file.} Bxb4 12. Rc1 $1 {It is hard for Black to avoid losing a piece now.} O-O 13. Bxc6 Bxb1 14. Bd4 $1 {This attack is devastating. The black queen has nowhere to run.} Qc7 15. Raxb1 Qe7 $1 {Desperately trying to cause trouble.} 16. Nd3 $1 {This ensures that the black queenside pawns will not remain intact.} ({If White meekly retreats with} 16. Ba4 {, Black at least has two passed pawns on the queenside for his piece.}) 16... Rfc8 $1 {Another resourceful defence in a dying position.} 17. Nxb4 a5 $1 {White is going to be a piece up here whatever happens. What is the most incisive way to realise his advantage?} 18. Bxf6 $1 gxf6 (18... Qxf6 19. Bxb7 Rxc1+ 20. Rxc1 Rb8 21. Na6 $1 Rxb7 22. Rc8+ {mates.}) 19. Nxd5 $1 exd5 20. Rxb7 $1 Qxb7 (20... Qe6 21. Qh5 Rxc6 ({or if} 21... f5 22. Rc3 {etc}) 22. Rxc6 Qxc6 23. Qxf7+ {mates.}) 21. Bxb7 Rxc1+ 22. Kf2 Rb8 23. Bxd5 Rb2 24. Qg4+ Kf8 25. Qd7 1-0

